@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Numeric Data Types";
}

<section>

    <!-- =====================================================
         PAGE INTRODUCTION
         ===================================================== -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Numeric Data Types
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            SQL Server provides different numeric data types to store
            whole numbers, decimal values and approximate numeric values.
            Choosing the correct numeric data type helps store data
            accurately and efficiently.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =====================================================
         WHAT ARE NUMERIC DATA TYPES?
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What are Numeric Data Types?
        </h3>

        <p>
            Numeric data types are used to store numbers in SQL Server.
            They can be used for values such as age, quantity, marks,
            salary, price, percentage and other numerical information.
        </p>

        <p>
            SQL Server provides several numeric data types depending
            on whether the value is a whole number or requires decimal
            precision.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         NUMERIC DATA TYPES TABLE
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common Numeric Data Types
        </h3>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Data Type</th>
                        <th>Description</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>tinyint</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            Stores small whole numbers from 0 to 255.
                        </td>
                        <td>100</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>smallint</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            Stores small-range whole numbers.
                        </td>
                        <td>15000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>int</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            Stores whole numbers and is commonly used
                            for identifiers and quantities.
                        </td>
                        <td>50000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>bigint</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            Stores very large whole numbers.
                        </td>
                        <td>9000000000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>decimal</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            Stores exact numeric values with fixed precision
                            and scale.
                        </td>
                        <td>12500.50</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>numeric</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            Equivalent to decimal and stores exact
                            numeric values.
                        </td>
                        <td>99.95</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>float</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            Stores approximate floating-point numbers.
                        </td>
                        <td>123.456</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>real</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            Stores approximate single-precision numbers.
                        </td>
                        <td>45.67</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         INTEGER DATA TYPES
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Integer Data Types
        </h3>

        <p>
            Integer data types are used when the value does not contain
            decimal places.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Data Type</th>
                        <th>Storage</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>tinyint</strong></td>
                        <td>1 byte</td>
                        <td>100</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>smallint</strong></td>
                        <td>2 bytes</td>
                        <td>15000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>int</strong></td>
                        <td>4 bytes</td>
                        <td>500000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>bigint</strong></td>
                        <td>8 bytes</td>
                        <td>9000000000</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         DECIMAL DATA TYPES
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Decimal and Exact Numeric Values
        </h3>

        <p>
            The <strong>decimal</strong> and <strong>numeric</strong>
            data types are used when exact decimal values are required.
            They are commonly used for financial and calculation-related
            values.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Products
(
    ProductId INT,
    ProductName VARCHAR(100),
    Price DECIMAL(10,2)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            In <strong>DECIMAL(10,2)</strong>, the first value represents
            the total precision and the second value represents the number
            of digits after the decimal point.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         EXAMPLE
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example: Using Numeric Data Types
        </h3>

        <p>
            The following example creates a Students table using
            different numeric data types.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    Age INT,
    Marks DECIMAL(5,2),
    Fees DECIMAL(10,2),
    TotalStudents BIGINT
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            Here, integer values are stored using integer data types,
            while values requiring decimal precision are stored using
            DECIMAL.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         OUTPUT
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Output
        </h3>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL Output
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>StudentId    Age    Marks    Fees
----------------------------------
1            21     85.50    45000.00
2            22     91.25    50000.00
3            20     78.75    40000.00</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            The output shows how SQL Server stores whole numbers and
            decimal values using appropriate numeric data types.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         CHOOSING CORRECT DATA TYPE
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Choosing the Correct Numeric Data Type
        </h3>

        <p>
            The numeric data type should be selected according to the
            size and type of value that needs to be stored.
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>INT</strong> for commonly used whole numbers.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>BIGINT</strong> for very large whole numbers.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>DECIMAL</strong> for exact decimal values.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>FLOAT</strong> when approximate numeric values
                are acceptable.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         SUMMARY
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned about SQL Server numeric
                data types such as TINYINT, SMALLINT, INT, BIGINT,
                DECIMAL, NUMERIC, FLOAT and REAL. We also learned how
                to choose an appropriate numeric data type according
                to the type and size of data being stored.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


